  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This specimen is a clear, minimal carrier layer with explicit dispatch boundaries, but for this scenario it mostly defers per-hop authorization failure semantics to unspecified wrapper or payload protocols, and its opaque-forwarding rule can permit relay without understanding authorization state.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit [pcid, payload] structure and no speculative parsing keep layer boundaries clear.",
      "Deterministic CBOR gives stable canonical bytes for hashing or signature wrappers.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling and wrapper-based extensibility support migration without a central registry."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "It does not define ring behavior for authorization failure: break, skip, refusal record, or membership reconfiguration.",
      "Per-hop authorization evidence has no mandatory slot or standard refusal artifact in the base envelope.",
      "Long-term audit depends on preserving external wrapper specs and handlers for this still-draft specimen."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A node may store-and-forward opaque envelopes without understanding whether forwarding is authorized for the next hop.",
      "Different wrapper conventions can represent refusal or hop authorization incompatibly across implementations.",
      "Authorization failures may collapse into silent drops instead of durable peer-local evidence."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What concrete wrapper or payload protocol records Bob's refusal to forward to Carol?",
      "Must a node understand the authorization wrapper before opaque store-and-forward is allowed?",
      "How would ring reconfiguration after an authorization failure be encoded and audited over time?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
